4.2. Case key Most of the time the case of a word (lower, upper or title) can be determined automatically in modern text input systems, particularly word-based systems. For example, in English the first word in a sentence and proper nouns are typically capitalized. However, there are exceptions to these normal rules. Automatic casing makes the use of the legacy Shift key unnecessary most of the time, but not all the time. This situation makes it difficult for the user to decide if to press the Shift key before entering a word. To correct the case of a word afterward with the Shift key is even more cumbersome because the user has to first select the text to be modified, delete it, and then use the Shift or CapsLock keys to trigger a mode change, and finally retype the text.
We introduced a new key on the keyboard, the Case key (see the lower left corner of Figure 1). This key cycles through the different word case alternatives for the word just entered or preceding the text caret. The Case key uses dictionary information to intelligently support nonstandard casing convention for some words, such as “iPhone”. Since the Case key modifies the word preceding the current text caret position (“reverse Polish”) it enables users to perform case corrections after the word is entered and only when they are actually needed.

